<p>Hail gets most of the attention in North Texas, but wind does just as much damage to Dallas roofs, fences, trees, and commercial buildings. A thunderstorm does not need to spin to tear shingles off a house in Lake Highlands or peel the membrane off a warehouse in Carrollton. Straight-line winds and downbursts hit Dallas County every year, sometimes with speeds that rival a strong tornado.</p>

<p>This guide covers how wind damage insurance claims work in Dallas, what the NOAA record shows about local wind events, the disputes that come up most often, and how to document a claim so it holds up.</p>

<h2>The kinds of wind that damage Dallas properties</h2>

<h3>Straight-line thunderstorm winds</h3>

<p>Most damaging wind in Dallas County comes from ordinary severe thunderstorms. Strong outflow races ahead of a storm line and can push gusts above 70 mph across miles of neighborhoods. When a line of storms organizes into a long, fast-moving system, the damage path can cover several cities at once, as it did across north Dallas County in May 2024.</p>

<h3>Downbursts and microbursts</h3>

<p>A downburst is a concentrated column of sinking air that slams into the ground and spreads out. A microburst is a small, intense version, often less than a few miles across. On May 8, 2014, a National Weather Service survey crew determined that a microburst with estimated 90 mph winds hit a neighborhood in the City of Dallas near Westmoreland Road and Canada Drive. The brick facade was torn off the front of a church, and nearby homes had roof, siding, and outbuilding damage.</p>

<h3>Tornado winds</h3>

<p>Tornadoes are a separate category in the NOAA record, with 109 Dallas County reports since 1952. For insurance purposes they are usually treated as windstorm, the same peril as straight-line wind. We cover them in detail in our guide to <a href="/tornado-damage-insurance-claims-dallas/">tornado damage insurance claims in Dallas</a>.</p>

