<h3>May 28, 2024: Garland, Richardson, and beyond</h3>

<p>The morning of May 28, 2024, is the most recent large wind event in Dallas County, and it shows how wide a straight-line wind event can be. NWS storm surveys found winds up to 95 mph in <a href="/service-areas/garland/">Garland</a>, where widespread tree, fence, and minor roof damage was consistent with 65 to 80 mph winds and some pockets reached 80 to 85 mph or more. In <a href="/service-areas/richardson/">Richardson</a>, a wind sensor at Methodist Richardson measured 83 mph, and the survey found winds up to 90 mph in part of the city, with local officials reporting 111 facilities with some degree of damage.</p>

<p>The same storm brought a 75 mph gust at Dallas Love Field, a 71 mph gust in Irving with widespread tree and roof damage, estimated 80 mph winds in Valley Ranch, a roof torn from a commercial building at Keller Springs and Midway in Carrollton, and a partial roof collapse in Pleasant Grove. For many households, it meant a roof claim, a fence claim, and a tree removal bill from a single morning.</p>

<h3>June 9, 2019: the crane collapse storm</h3>

<p>On June 9, 2019, thunderstorm winds swept across the county. Dallas Love Field recorded a 71 mph gust, with power poles, billboards, and roof damage reported nearby. Large trees went down along I-635 between Hillcrest and Coit, and winds caused a construction crane to collapse onto an apartment complex in Dallas, severely damaging an adjoining parking garage. Events like this one often produce claims from apartment owners, condo associations, and businesses as well as homeowners.</p>

