<h2>What wind does to Dallas buildings</h2>

<h3>Composition shingle roofs</h3>

<p>Most Dallas houses are brick veneer with composition shingle roofs. Strong gusts can lift shingle tabs, break the adhesive seal strip, and crease the shingle along the nail line. A creased shingle may lie flat again after the storm and look fine from the street, yet it no longer seals and can blow off in the next storm. Missing shingles, exposed nail heads, and shingle debris in the yard are the obvious signs; creasing is the one that gets missed. Ridge caps, hip shingles, and the edges of rakes and eaves tend to fail first.</p>

<h3>Low-slope commercial roofs</h3>

<p>Apartments, retail centers, churches, and warehouses across Dallas County often have flat or low-slope TPO, modified-bitumen, or metal roofs. Wind can lift the membrane at the perimeter, pull flashing loose, and damage rooftop HVAC units. Once a membrane is lifted, it can trap moisture in the insulation below, so a moisture survey may be needed to see the full extent. The NOAA record includes several Dallas County buildings with roofs peeled away, from Lancaster to Farmers Branch to Carrollton. Our page on <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> covers business income and other issues specific to these losses.</p>

<h3>Trees, fences, and other structures</h3>

<p>Dallas neighborhoods like Lakewood, Kessler Park, and the Park Cities have large, mature trees. In a downburst, limbs come down on roofs, cars, and fences. Wood privacy fences are often the first casualty. Fences, detached garages, and sheds generally fall under the other structures coverage on a homeowners policy, while tree removal coverage is often subject to a smaller sub-limit.</p>

<h3>Water that follows wind</h3>

<p>Many policies cover rain that enters through an opening created by wind, such as a missing section of roof or a broken window. Rain that seeps in through an old, worn roof without a storm opening is a different story. Photograph the opening and the interior damage together, and dry the home quickly to limit mold.</p>

<h2>How wind claims are handled</h2>

<h3>Your wind/hail deductible</h3>

<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often 1% or 2% of the dwelling (Coverage A) limit. On a home insured for $400,000, a 2% deductible is $8,000. That number decides whether a fence-only or minor shingle claim makes financial sense, so find it on your declarations page before you need it.</p>

<h3>Common disputes</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Wear and tear.</strong> Insurers may attribute lifted or missing shingles to age, poor installation, or deterioration rather than the storm.</li>
<li><strong>Wind versus hail.</strong> Dallas storms often bring both. The cause can affect how damage is scoped, and in some cases which deductible or exclusion applies.</li>
<li><strong>Repair versus replace.</strong> A carrier may offer to replace a handful of shingles when the roof cannot be repaired without damaging surrounding shingles, or when matching materials are not available.</li>
<li><strong>Missed items.</strong> Ridge caps, drip edge, flashing, gutters, and interior ceiling repairs are often left out of the first estimate. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">reviewing an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a> covers what to look for.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Prompt payment timelines</h3>

<p>Under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act, an insurer generally has 15 days after notice to acknowledge the claim and begin its investigation, then 15 business days after receiving all requested information to accept or reject it. It can notify you that it needs more time, up to 45 additional days. After a declared weather catastrophe, the deadlines can be extended by 15 days. Keep a written log of every request and response.</p>

<h2>How to document a wind damage claim</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Note the date and time</strong> of the storm, and save the NOAA or National Weather Service report. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> helps you find events near your address.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph from the ground first:</strong> every roof slope, ridge lines, missing shingles, debris in the yard, damaged fences, and fallen limbs.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph inside:</strong> ceiling stains, wet insulation, broken windows, and anything damaged by falling debris.</li>
<li><strong>Make temporary repairs</strong> such as tarping and boarding up, and keep receipts. Most policies require you to protect the property from further damage.</li>
<li><strong>Keep damaged materials</strong> when practical, such as a blown-off shingle or a section of fence.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong>, and ask that the inspection include the roof, exterior, and interior.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When to get help with a wind claim</h2>

<p>Simple claims sometimes resolve without outside help. If the estimate looks thin, the insurer blames wear and tear, or you have a commercial loss with business interruption, a licensed public adjuster may be useful. Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps Dallas County homeowners and businesses with <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a> by inspecting the property, documenting the damage pattern, preparing a detailed Xactimate estimate, and presenting the claim. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Results depend on your policy and the facts of the loss.</p>
